/*
 * A rank-up keeps the experience the character had already accumulated instead
 * of emptying the bar -- written as the presence of a file:
 * custom_mods\rank_up_keep_progress_on under the game folder, beside
 * Rance10.exe. Delete it and both rank-ups go back to rounding the progress
 * down to nothing. It is read as the rank is awarded, so neither takes a
 * restart.
 *
 * WHAT THE GAME DOES. Two places hand a character exactly the experience
 * missing from the next rank and not one point more:
 *
 *     SceneQuestMapExp@ShowExpUpEffect (23856)   the ＥＸＰ tile on a quest map
 *         exp = CalcRankUpExp(c.Star) - c.Exp
 *     SkillEffectProcessRankUp@Process (27510)   the in-battle rank-up effect
 *         c.AddExp(c.NextExp - c.Exp)
 *
 * Both land the character on the next rank with an empty bar, so the further
 * along the bar was, the less the rank-up was worth: a character at 99% of the
 * way to the next rank collects a single point of experience from it, and the
 * player who wants full value has to spend the tile on whoever is closest to
 * having just ranked up. docs/battle-experience.md has both call chains and
 * what else reads these numbers.
 *
 * WHAT THIS CHANGES. Only the subtrahend. With the switch on this returns 0
 * where the game reads c.Exp, so the two expressions become CalcRankUpExp(
 * c.Star) and c.NextExp -- the full price of the rank, handed over on top of
 * what the character had rather than instead of it. Character@AddExp (27962)
 * then spends that on the ranks and leaves the remainder in the bar, and the
 * remainder is exactly the old c.Exp:
 *
 *     rank 22, 545/814 (67%)   ->   rank 23, 545/895 (61%)
 *
 * Points carry, not the percentage, because each rank costs 1.1x the one below
 * it (Character::CalcNextExp is min(99999, 100 * 1.1^star)). Holding the
 * percentage instead would mean carrying a ratio through float arithmetic at
 * both sites; holding the points needs no arithmetic at all, which is why this
 * is one returned number rather than a rewrite of the two expressions.
 * docs/rank-up-keep-progress.md compares the two.
 *
 * The carry cannot overshoot into an extra rank, and that is a property of
 * Character@AddExp rather than of this file: it only ever leaves a remainder
 * smaller than the rank it just paid for, so c.Exp < c.NextExp holds going in
 * and coming out. It also composes with MapExpBonusUp, the clear-point bonus
 * that buys how many ranks the tile is worth (docs/clear-point-bonuses.md) --
 * AddExp spends the whole sum rank by rank and the same remainder survives at
 * the end of it.
 *
 * WHY THE SUBTRACTION SURVIVES. Deleting the SUB would say the same thing in
 * fewer instructions, but a deleted instruction cannot be put back by a file in
 * custom_mods, and the .ain would carry the change for everybody. Subtracting a
 * zero this function chooses leaves the switched-off path doing
 * instruction-for-instruction what the original did, which is the property
 * features/always-first-finisher gets out of super().
 *
 * WHY A .jaf AND TWO .jam. This half is the switch, and it is a .jaf because it
 * wants HLL calls and a string literal and nothing off the .ain at all. The
 * other half has to reach inside two existing function bodies, which
 * alice-tools' .jaf override cannot do -- features/reliable-cooking/
 * cooking_skill_chance.jam has the error message you get for trying, and the
 * reason: an override resolves neither `this` nor a struct's own members. Two
 * .jam files rather than one because every .jam in this repository carries
 * exactly one FUNC ... ENDFUNC block, and these two sites have nothing in
 * common but the call below.
 *
 * The path is spelled with forward slashes because the .jaf lexer does not take
 * a backslash in a string literal at all, and Windows accepts either separator.
 * It is looked for twice for the reason features/enemy-panel/enemy_info_panel.jaf
 * gives: whether SystemService.GetGameFolderPath() ends in a separator is
 * something the game does not agree with itself about, and trying both
 * spellings settles it without running the game. A path that does not resolve
 * reads as "no file", which is the harmless direction -- the game behaving
 * exactly as it does unpatched.
 */
int RankUpExpToConsume(int exp)
{
	string folder = SystemService.GetGameFolderPath();
	if (FileOperation.ExistFile(folder + "custom_mods/rank_up_keep_progress_on")
	 || FileOperation.ExistFile(folder + "/custom_mods/rank_up_keep_progress_on")) {
		return 0;
	}
	return exp;
}
